The findings show that bubble and selection sort were consistently the worst performing. The data has some small aberrations with every sorting algorithm for every array, likely due to background tasks being run on the machine. More surpsising is that the data shows insertion sort performing consistently much better than merge sort. While I'm not certain what the cause of this is, I believe there might be some more constant overhead involved with the algorithm's implementation or my machine specifically that causes the merge sort algorithm to run slower. Arrays.sort and quicksort both ran comparably quickly. Quicksort seemed to scale better for the unsorted array, while Arrays.sort scaled better for all the other arrays. It seems that Arrays.sort is likely base on quicksort (after looking at documentation, it seems Arrays.sort uses quicksort for arrays with primitive types and a modified merge sort algorithm for arrays of objects; the typesafe implementation of Sorting.java I wrote uses object arrays, so the runtime data might be different if primitive types are used).
